/**
 * What every Odin feature does, in the user's terms: the raven's map of Odin,
 * served by `/odin` action=guide. He answers from this and the live state,
 * never from Odin's source. A feature you add or change belongs here too:
 * odin-guide-coverage.test.ts and CI's guide job (scripts/guide-check.ts)
 * fail a PR that leaves one out.
 */
export const ODIN_GUIDE = `# Odin

"Store:" names are for your own action=state calls. Never show them, or any other code, to the user.

## Left rail
Top to bottom: Dev Board, Tasks, Automations, Night Agent (status with its settings - the on/off switch, the window and the night's cap are edited right in the header - Last night - the sessions the last window started and where each stands now, click one to open it - the next run's picks - add, reorder, remove, and what to do after them - its log by night, and its instructions), More (the ⋮ button: Review, Insights, Session History). At the foot: Settings, and the raven button (Huginn, ⌘⇧I). Each but Night Agent has a one-key hotkey (rebind in Settings → Keyboard); the keys do nothing while you type, also inside agent terminals.

## Tasks screen and its feeds
Tabs, in two groups split by a hairline: Odin's own (All, Tasks, Docs), then the connections (Slack, Jira, GitHub, Notion, Email, ClickUp, Calendar).
- **Badges** count only what waits on you: Slack counts only "Not started" rows, GitHub leaves out bot PRs, Email leaves out junk, Done rows never count. All is the sum of the others, minus your own PRs.
- **Amber dot** on a tab: that source is not connected. **Red dot**: connected but failing.
- Feeds reload every 2 minutes. **Sync** refreshes the connected sources.
- **Columns**: Task, Sweep, Priority, Status, From, Where, a date.
- **Row actions**: Open in <source>, due date, "Line it up - Up next" / "Take it off Up next", "Stop waiting - back on top of Up next" (the hourglass, on a task Waiting on others), Tonight (moon, picks it for the Night Agent), "Mark it Read later - it moves to Docs", **Drop** (the ban icon, also in the side panel and on a Next in line card's hover: what Review's Drop does, no sweep needed - Done everywhere, listed under Review's Dropped, Undo on the toast), **Start** ("Go to session" when a session already runs on it), **Done**. A task with a session running says **Working** next to its title (**Needs you** when the session is waiting on you), with a **+** beside it, "Start another session": a task can have several sessions, all linked to it. With more than one open the row says "Working · N sessions", and Go to session opens the newest. Done on a session marks its task Done, unless another session on that task is still open; Undo brings both back.
- **Docs** lists saved docs (~/.odin/docs), sessions moved there off the board (Back to board resumes one, Forget drops the row), and Read later rows (Back to Tasks).
- **Done** is Odin only: nothing is written back to the source, and Undo is offered. A row done in one place leaves every feed. Store: done.
- **Same task, several feeds**: on All, a Jira ticket, the Notion page that copied it and the Slack message asking about it are one row when they share a Jira key or PR link, or the same title (four words or more). The owner's row stays (Jira, GitHub, ClickUp, Notion, Tasks, Slack, Email, in that order) with a chip per other source that opens it there; Done puts them all away. A reworded copy stays its own row.
- **Right-click a task row** (All and every feed tab): the row's own actions first - "Start session" (or "Go to session"), "Done", "Open in <source>", "Line it up - top of Up next" / "Line it up - end of Up next" (on Up next: "Move to the top of Up next", "Take it off Up next"; waiting: "Stop waiting - back on top of Up next"), "Waiting on someone else" (Back on Up next tomorrow, in 3 days, in a week), "Set a due date" (Today, Tomorrow, In a week, or Drop the due date; any other day is the row's calendar icon), "Pick for tonight", "Mark it Read later", "Drop it". With several rows ticked on All, right-clicking one of them acts on every ticked row, like the bar: "Start N sessions", "Done - N tasks", "Line N up - top of Up next" / "end of Up next", "Waiting on someone else", "Set a due date on N", "Clear selection".
- **Right-click a row** (any feed tab, or Review): "Drop <source> tasks like this from now on" - From <person>, In <repo/channel/project>, or Titled <exact title>. Matching rows are marked Done now and as they arrive. The rules list under All's Filter, "Dropped automatically"; × removes one and brings its rows back. Store: odin.all.dropRules.
- **Start** opens "Start: <title>": click Start session, or "+ Add context" for guidelines or images first. The session opens on the Dev Board, linked to the task (the button turns into Go to session while it runs). Hovering Start says this. While a session runs, the side panel has Go to session and **Start another session**.
- **Side panel** (click a task's title): labelled fields Status, Type and Priority, plus "+ Up next". Every task has a Notes box there: your own tasks' notes are their brief; on a Slack, Jira, GitHub, Notion, Email, ClickUp or Calendar row, Notes sit under the source's own text, stay in Odin only, and are added to the brief when you Start session.
- **Status** is Odin's own if you set one (Backlog, Prioritised, In progress, In review, On hold), else the source's. Odin's status stays in Odin.
- **Priority** maps to high, medium or low. A Slack reaction and a Jira @-mention always count as High.

## Tasks (your own backlog)
"my own list · start a session when you're ready". Type in "What needs doing?" and click Add task.
- The first line is the title, the rest is the brief.
- A leading "!" is Low, "!!!" is High, none is Medium. The Priority picker writes the same marks.
- A "/skill" after the "!"s (or the Skill picker) makes the session start by running that skill with the title as its argument.
- The Repo picker ("agent picks") sets the checkout. Empty: the agent picks the repo from the brief.
- More fields under the box (on Tasks → All and in the New task dialog): **Up next** (No, Start of queue, End of queue), **Due** (a date), **Status** and **Type**, the same ones the side panel sets.
- Sorted by priority, then newest first. Starting a task keeps it on the list, linked to its session: started is not finished.
- Schedules (automations) don't show here. Each profile has its own list. The New task dialog has a Start now box.
- Any Claude session Odin starts can add a task here: ask it to "open an Odin task" and it adds one with a title, notes, a due date and an Up next place if you give them.
- Store: tasks.

## Next in line (Dev Board column)
Tasks waiting to start, from Tasks, Slack, Jira, GitHub and Notion only (not Email, ClickUp or Calendar). Anything with a live session, ever started, or Done drops out.
- "Newest first" until you click **Sort with AI**; then it reads "Sorted by AI · undo" with Re-sort. The sort words live in Settings → Backlog ("How to sort it"). Rows the AI hides come back with "show hidden (N)".
- Sections: **Tonight** (your picks, at most 20), then **Due** (dated tasks; overdue ones stay pinned up to 30 days by default, "Pin overdue tasks for"), then **Everything else**.
- Suspected duplicates are dimmed, and the Night Agent skips them: the same ticket, PR link or title as a session already run, or one still open, including a session on one of your own tasks.
- Store: nightAgent.

## Slack feed
- React to a Slack message with the **Queue** emoji (default :eyes:) to bring it in. The **Auto-start** emoji (default :robot_face:) brings it in and starts a session at once. With Settings → Remote → Auto-start new tasks → Slack on and the remote host on, the Queue emoji starts a session on the host too. Click either chip to change its emoji. The row is named by Claude (Haiku) from the message and its thread, a few words on what it's about; until that lands, or when Claude isn't there, it shows the message's first line. Your own rename always wins.
- Pills: Not started (no session ever), In progress (a session was started once), Done. It opens on the first pill with rows. Search matches the author only.
- Removing the reaction in Slack does not remove the row; Review's sweep can drop it.

## Jira feed
Open issues assigned to you, reported by you, or with a comment that @-mentions you. Pills: Assigned to me, Reported by me, Mentioning me, All (default; leaves out issues you only reported). Group by Sprints (default) or Projects (by epic, "No epic" last). Project filter and search. In Progress first, live sessions first, parked statuses (hold, blocked) last.

## GitHub feed
Open PRs. Pills: To review (default), Mine, Mentions. Bot PRs are hidden ("show bot PRs (N)"). Repo filter. Your own PRs don't count in All.

## Notion feed
Pick a database; turn on "@ Mentions" to add pages assigned to you and comment threads that @-mention you. Grouped by status, finished last. Odin sees only pages and databases shared with its integration.

## Email feed
Gmail inbox mail that was unread when Odin first saw it; it stays after you open it, until you mark it Done. Pills: Interesting (default) and All. Junk shows only under All; the model decides junk, and every calendar mail is junk. Right-click a mail, then Ignore mail like this: its sender is junk from then on, and the model treats look-alikes as junk too. Read-only.

## ClickUp feed
Open ClickUp tasks assigned to you. No filters. Done on a ClickUp task (here, on All, or ticked with others) also closes it in ClickUp: it moves to its list's closed status (or its done status when the list has no closed one). Only that way: Undo, or bringing it back from "Show what's Done", leaves ClickUp as it is. When ClickUp can't be updated, the task stays Done in Odin and a toast says "ClickUp wasn't updated" with the reason. Reading material doesn't touch ClickUp.

## Calendar feed
Meetings from now to the end of tomorrow, soonest first, each one a task of type "Prepare for meeting". A meeting is due the day it happens, so today's sit in Now on All and tomorrow's in Later. The preview line shows the time and who is in it. Open joins the call (Meet, Zoom, Teams or Webex) in your own browser. Start session hands the agent the agenda, organizer and attendees to prepare from. Done puts away that one occurrence, not the rest of a series. Left out: all-day events, cancelled ones, ones you declined, and blocks with nobody else in them. A meeting never merges with another feed's row. Read-only; polled every 5 minutes.

## Review (rail, More menu)
"Sweep now" checks every backlog item (your tasks plus Slack, Jira, GitHub and Notion rows) against its source. It also sweeps on its own, every hour by default ("Sweep the backlog every" in Settings → Backlog; 0 turns it off).
- Verdicts: DROP, KEEP or UNKNOWN. DROP needs evidence: a merged or closed PR, a Jira ticket in a done status, the :eyes: removed in Slack, a thread someone else answered, or 21 days without activity. What it couldn't reach is UNKNOWN.
- Pills: Drop, Keep & unknown, Dropped. Keep and Drop per row, "Drop all N" for the rows on screen.
- Drop marks the row Done; on a task it also deletes the task, on a Slack row it marks the message done. Drop is off while a session still works on the row.
- Dropped lists what was dropped here and by hand from any row (evidence "You dropped this") - a by-hand Drop marks a task Done rather than deleting it, so Undo brings it back.
- Store: backlogReview.

## Dev Board columns
Four columns: **Working** (blue), **Needs you** (amber), **Final Review** (green, "just done"), **Idle** (grey).
- Needs you: sessions waiting on a prompt or question, failed sessions ("✗ failed - click to see what broke"), and live sessions whose status was cleared.
- Final Review: cards whose turn ended without asking anything.
- A dead session goes to Idle, except one the board closed for being idle: it keeps its column.
- PR state moves cards: while CI runs on a card's PR it shows in Working with "CI running". When a check then fails on one of its open PRs, Odin sends the session the failed check names and the failed step's log, so it fixes them and pushes again: typed at its prompt when Claude sits idle there, or resumed with it when the session was closed. Once per red run (head commit); a red run from before this feature first ran is only recorded. After 3 red runs in a row the card goes to Needs you with "CI still red"; a green run, a merge or a close ends the streak. Store: odin-ci-wake. It moves from Needs you to Final Review when all that's left is merging approved PRs with no merge conflicts, a look, or its PRs were closed. It moves back to Needs you when the brief judges the latest reply asks you something.
- Sections in a column, in order: Starred, Recently finished (turn ended in the last 10 minutes while Odin was focused), Queued, Waiting on others, Parked, then Slack / Jira / GitHub / Notion / Normal.
- A starred card wears ★ with an amber left edge and tint, like a starred row in the list view.
- **Catch up** shows on Needs you and Final Review with more than 5 cards: one card at a time, "N Left", Next and ✓ Done.
- Drag a card to Idle to park it (a running turn gets Ctrl+C first). The session stays open.
- Undo is ⌘Z or the toast's Undo, for actions in the current board visit only.

## Board header
- **+ New Session**, **Search sessions** (title, brief, tags, person, repo, PRs), and a filter (Starred / Tags / Repos / People).
- **Next in line** toggle shows that column; ▶ Start launches a row, the moon picks it for tonight.
- **Reminders strip**: reminders whose day came and unread "New reply" pings, with Resume/Open, Snooze and ✕. Hover a reminder to see its whole text; you can select and copy it there or in the strip.

## Cards
- Hover: ☆ Star (pins to top), ✓ Done (always visible in Final Review), the bell Remind me (tomorrow at the earliest).
- Right-click: Star, Done, "Move to Idle", Remind me…, the Ping me items, then More (folded: Move to Docs, Restart session, the remote host moves, VS Code on the host, Remote Control, Save as doc) and Tags. Details: "Move to Docs" (off the board like Done, listed under Docs → Sessions, where the title reads the transcript and Back to board resumes it; Undo brings the card back), Remind me…, "Ping me" (pings on new replies when the session has a Slack or Jira thread, and when its PR gets approved; while nothing new came in, its Needs you or Done card waits in Idle under "Waiting on others", and a reply or approval brings it back to Needs you), "Ping me when…" (you type what to watch for; the agent in the session checks it once an hour and pings you when it happens), "Stop watching" (on a session with a "Ping me when…" watch: drops it and clears its Happened ping; while it still runs, also asks the agent to cancel its scheduled checks), "Turn on Remote Control" (when Settings is on "Selected sessions"), "Run on remote host" (a session on this computer, when a remote host is set up: pushes its branch, committing uncommitted work first in a session worktree, and resumes the conversation on the host, told to check that branch out), "Run on this computer" (the reverse, for a session on the host), "Open in VS Code on the host" (opens the session's folder on the host in VS Code over Remote-SSH with your own setup: your settings, your local extensions installed there, and, when your shell is zsh, your .zshrc/.zprofile/.zshenv in its terminal; a VS Code window left on the host reconnects by itself when Odin starts, and an expired AWS sign-in opens the approval page), Keep (overrides a sweep's Drop), Tags (add your own).
- Pills: PR #N, "CI running", "Merge conflicts" (one of its open PRs conflicts with its base), "Just merge #N", "Review #N", "Dropped: PR merged/closed", "Duplicate? Same ask as…", Loop (a /loop session, with a countdown), a flame for a heavy session, Shell / Shell stopped / Shell exited, Automation, Night Agent, Auto-started.
- Idle cards have **Resume**, with "⏸ died mid-turn" or the exit cause. A session that died mid-turn is resumed on its own, once per run of the app. So is one whose Claude ended with a /loop still armed: it reopens at an idle prompt and the loop runs again. So is a remote-host session whose pane here died while its Claude still runs in tmux on the host: it reattaches without a click (one check per host for all its cards). One whose tmux ended there, whose host didn't answer, or that idle close closed keeps Resume.
- A remote-host session runs under its own memory cap on the host: half the host's RAM, no swap, through the host user's systemd, with nothing to set up. A runaway build in it hits that cap and its biggest process is killed, instead of freezing the other sessions there. A host where that isn't available runs the session uncapped.
- When a session on the remote host gives you a command to run on your own computer, its reply has a **Run on my computer** button, and the chat doesn't show the command itself: in its place a line says it runs on your computer, not on the remote host. Hover the button to read the command. It runs in the session's Shell on this computer, and its output goes back to the session.
- **GSD Mode** (Get-Shit-Done Mode, as its hover says), beside the model under a session's reply box, gets the job done without you: right when you turn it on, Odin tells Claude to stop waiting for you - to pick the answer it would recommend for every open action item or choice, decide what it would ask you, and keep working until the job is done. Odin holds Claude to it rather than only asking: when a turn ends on action items Claude can still move (anything but a "(you only)" item or a move to your computer), Odin hands them back to it to do or decide, up to 3 times in a row, then stops and says so on the red line; a question Claude asks in a menu gets its first option, a plan it wants approved is approved, and a yes/no check in the terminal (a permission, folder trust) gets its Yes - each with a short notice. It also turns off the clicks for that session: when a turn ends (or right when you turn it on), Odin first shows a warning listing what it is about to run, with **Stop**, **Go** and a countdown, and 30 seconds later (or right away on **Go**) runs the reply's commands by itself (Run, Run on my computer, Download - not View on my computer), one after another, then sends their output to Claude in one message so it carries on. Nobody is there to answer a command, so GSD Mode never waits on one: a Run on my computer command gets no input and no pager (a prompt fails right away instead of waiting; git and gh do not prompt or open an editor), and one that prints nothing for 5 minutes is stopped - Ctrl+C, then its Shell closed - and Claude gets what it printed and is told to do it another way. A red line above the reply box says it is on, with **Turn off**, and what it is doing: the command it runs now ("Running 2 of 3 on your computer: ...") and for how many seconds, past 30 seconds with a note that Odin stops it if it prints nothing for 5 minutes; else that Claude is working, where it stopped and why, or that nothing is left to run and Claude stopped. It goes off when the session ends or Odin restarts, and works while the session's chat is open. Whatever that session writes then runs on your computer without a check, so turn it on only for a session you trust.
- When a session on the remote host hands you a file it made there (a CSV, a report, a log), its reply has a **Download:** button with the file name: it copies the file from the host to this computer's Downloads folder (as "name (2).csv" when that name is taken) and shows it in Finder. The phone app does not show it.
- When a session on the remote host runs an app with a screen there (a desktop app on a virtual display) and you want to see it, its reply has a **View on my computer** button: Odin tunnels to the app's screen over the host connection (no open port, no VS Code needed) and shows it in the session's drawer in the terminal's place, like the diff, signed in with the session's one-time password. From then on the drawer header has a ▣ View button that switches between the app and the session, and while the app shows, the footer has a button back to the view you last used (Chat View or Terminal View) that takes the app off screen; if the connection drops, Reconnect brings it back. To see the app in its own window you can move and resize, click ↗ Open in window at the top right of the View tab: the drawer goes back to the session, and ▣ View shows the app in the drawer again. It shows the host's screen compressed, so it stays responsive over the host connection, with some blur while things move. Copy and paste work both ways: what you copy in the app lands on your clipboard, and Cmd+V (Ctrl+V off a Mac) pastes your clipboard into it; on a Mac, Cmd+C, Cmd+X, Cmd+A and Cmd+Z work in the app too. Plain text only, and characters outside Western European ones paste as ?. A later reply that asks you to click it shows the latest such button in its own Action Items card. The phone app does not show it.
- A session that changes something you can see puts screenshots on its PR as you: Odin on this computer opens the PR in a hidden window of its own browser and uploads them through the comment box, as a drag and drop would, and the session posts them in a comment. The first time, if Odin's browser isn't signed in to GitHub, a "Sign in to GitHub for Odin" window opens; sign in there once. It needs this computer awake: a session on the remote host with the laptop closed says so and gives you the files instead. Ask a session for the files and it gives them to you (from the remote host, with a **Download:** button).
- When a session on the remote host is blocked on something only your computer has (and a Run on my computer command can't do it), its action items ask to move it here, and that item has a **Move** button: like Move to this computer, it brings the conversation and its code to this computer and continues it here.
- When the remote host runs out of memory (under 0.5 GB free, counting the host's cap on its users' memory), its sessions freeze: Claude stops drawing and typing seems to do nothing. The drawer of a session there then shows a red line saying so, with **Move to this computer**, which continues the conversation here.
- When a session's remote host is down (stopped, terminated, or out of AWS's reach) and the remote host new sessions go to is another instance that is up, the "Could not reconnect" line in its chat shows **Move to <that host>**: the conversation (and its code, if the old host still answers) moves to that host and resumes there.
- A remote session that loses its connection reconnects by itself, waiting longer between tries (after 3 failed tries, pressing R in its terminal reboots the host). A login or attach that never answers counts as a failed try after 3 minutes. After 6 failed tries in a row it stops and says so in a red box in the chat: "Stopped reconnecting to <host> after 6 tries", the reason on the line below, "Trying again by itself at <time>." (it tries once more on its own after 5 minutes, then waits twice as long after each miss, up to every 30 minutes), and under it **Retry** (try again), **Start over on the host** (ends the session's terminal on the host and starts Claude there again, resuming the conversation), **Move to <host>** (when the remote host new sessions go to is another instance that is up, even if this session's host is up too) and **Move to this computer**. In its terminal the same choices are Enter, F and R (reboot the host).
- When one of a host's sessions gets back in after a lost connection, the others on that host waiting to reconnect (stopped ones too) try again within a few seconds instead of waiting out their own time.
- When two or more sessions on one remote host can't log in, a red line over the Dev Board says "<host> isn't taking logins: N sessions there can't reconnect", with **Reboot <host>**: one reboot for all of them. A minute later every session there reconnects by itself and resumes its conversation.
- Odin's host setup caps all sessions on a host together at 80% of its memory and keeps the host's SSH and SSM running when memory runs out, so a host whose sessions use too much stays reachable. Settings' connection check ("Memory and disk") warns when a host doesn't have that cap yet, with **Set up** to add it.
- While sessions run on the remote host, the top bar's status pill lists it on hover ("<host>: N sessions. It has X GB free."); the bar itself shows it only yellow with "launches there wait" when the host is short of room, or red with "out of memory · sessions frozen" when its sessions are frozen.
- **Done** ends the session: stops the process, removes the card and marks its feed row done. Its Undo resumes the same conversation.
- **Idle close**: a session idle longer than 3 hours (Settings → Sessions, "Close idle sessions after", 0 = never) is closed; Resume reopens it. Never closed: remote sessions (they hold nothing on this computer), working, queued, looping, waiting on CI, or a busy shell. Idle time counts from the later of the last status and the last Resume. Store: idleClose.
- **Idle compact**: a session whose card moves into Idle - parked there by drag or "Move to Idle", or put under "Waiting on others" by Ping me - gets /compact typed into it right away (Settings → Sessions, "Compact conversations moved to Idle", off by default), so its next reply starts from the summary while Claude's cache is still warm. Once per turn; never a looping or queued session, and one whose drawer is open waits until the drawer closes. Done and Needs you cards, and a session already in Idle when Odin starts, are left alone. The card shows Compacting in Working and comes back where it was. Store: idleCompact.
- Queued cards show "⏳ <reason>" and **Start now**, which ignores the gate. A card waiting on the remote host has no Start now: its **Start on this computer** button (also under the wait in the drawer) starts it on this computer instead. Cards further back read "#N in line, after "X"". The queue starts the longest-waiting launch that can go. A Resume that opens at an idle prompt is never held.

## Sessions view and the session drawer
- **Inbox view**, the third icon (an inbox) beside Sessions view and Board view in the top bar: every open action item from the Needs you and Final Review sessions in one list, so you clear them without opening each card. Items sit in three bands by how long they take you: **One click** (a choice, an Approve, a merge, a ! command), **Needs a look** (a PR, a draft, a page to check) and **Needs thinking** (an open question, a "(you only)" chore, Move to my computer). Each row shows the item and its session's title, and hovering it shows the task (the session's opening ask) and where it stands (the part of the session's last reply about that item); a choice or Approve button on the row answers the session right there (typed in while it runs, else it resumes with the answer) and the row goes away; clicking the item's text shows that session's Brief beside the list, so the Inbox stays in view and clicking another row switches the Brief; **Open session** on the Brief, or **Open** on the row, opens the session, and closing it brings you back to the Inbox. On top, **Stuck** lists sessions that can't move on their own and aren't asking, each with one fix: CI still red after its fixes (Open), Failed (Open), Died mid-turn (Resume), queued over 30 minutes (Resume), PR conflicts with main (Rebase on main - asks the session to rebase and push), working with no new output for 45 minutes (Ask for status - asks the session for a two-line status).
- The Sessions and Board icons in the board header switches Board view and Sessions view (hotkey O). Sessions view opens the first session in flight, Needs you first; off when nothing is in flight. Its side list sorts by Needs you first / Last update / Waiting longest / Name, has Group, folds Idle away. ⌘⌥↑ / ⌘⌥↓ walk it. The open session stays in view in the list: when it changes state and moves to another group, the list scrolls to it.
- Drawer header: click the title to rename; source link pill; ★, ▣ View (on a remote session that shared an app's screen with a View on my computer button), ⑂ Diff, ❯ Shell, ⓘ Brief (open by default), ⛶ full width.
- Drawer footer: ↻ Resume / Continue / ▶ Start now, ■ Interrupt (Ctrl+C, terminal view only), Chat View, ✓ Done, Remind me, Minimize Session. Esc on a session screen stops the agent's running turn, the same as the stop button (the screen stays open); with nothing running, Esc closes the drawer and goes back to the board. Esc still closes an open dialog, menu, ⌘F find bar or Claude's own menu first. Rebind or turn off the stop as "Stop the turn" in Settings → Keyboard (record another key, or press Esc while recording to unassign it; Reset brings back Esc); unassigned, Esc only goes back to the board.
- Continue types "Continue" into a live session (off while it works). Resume on a dead session reopens the same conversation at an idle prompt. If the transcript is gone: "That conversation is gone", offering a new session from its brief (a remote session asks the host first - its transcript here is only a mirror). A bulk Resume over ticked rows takes that offer by itself and says "Started over on".
- **Plan limit**: while claude-auto keeps this computer on the API key after the plan's limit (~/.claude/plan-limit.json), a yellow line under the drawer header says "API key until Wed 06:00 · plan limit" (hover for the limit Claude printed), and a Restart's toast says it came back on the key. **Use plan** deletes that file, as claude-auto --use-plan does, and restarts the session; it fails again if the limit still holds. Without claude-auto there is no file and no line.
- **API error retry**: when a session's turn fails on an API error that passes on its own (a Cloudflare "Attention Required" page, which Claude Code shows as "Please run /login", a 5xx, overloaded, a dropped connection or a timeout), Odin waits 30 seconds, toasts "API error - retrying the turn" and types a retry into the session. After 3 retries in a row it stops and the card stays in Needs you as failed. A real sign-in error (Not logged in, 401) is not retried.
- **Diff**: the working tree, the branch's commits since it left main (no PR needed), or any PR the session opened. Comment on lines, then "➤ send N comments to agent".
- **Brief**: "PR #N has merge conflicts with main" at the top while one of its open PRs conflicts, Goal, Your action items (from the reply's ACTION ITEMS), Where it stands, links (the PRs, threads and pages the session linked, the ones you named in your messages or added, and those on the Jira ticket it is about - its description, people's comments and remote links - whoever wrote them), token and cost figures, My notes (⌘Enter adds a note to your to-dos).

## Chat view and terminal view
Chat view is the default (Settings → Appearance, "Show sessions as a chat"; store sessionView). Switch with "Terminal View" in the composer or "Chat View" in the footer (while a ▣ View app covers the session, the footer button names the view under it, the one last used, and takes the app off screen). Same session behind both; a question or plan approval still opens the terminal.
- Composer: Enter sends, Ctrl+Enter sends into a running turn, Shift+Enter new line, "!" bash mode, "/" skills, hold Space to talk.
- **Model** picker in the composer, beside "Terminal View": shows the model the session's latest reply ran on (Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, or the model's own name). Pick another and Odin sends "/model <name>" to the session; the next reply runs on it, and Claude Code also keeps it as the default for new sessions. Off while a turn runs, and on an ended session (resume it to change). Shown once the session has replied.
- Claude's menus show as clickable cards.
- Find in the conversation: ⌘F (Ctrl+F on Windows and Linux) with the chat focused or last clicked opens a "Find in conversation" bar at its top right. Matches highlight as you type, with a count (2/7, or No matches); Enter and Shift+Enter (or the up and down arrows) move between them and scroll each into view; Esc or the X closes it. It searches the turns loaded so far (scroll up to load earlier ones) and a shortened reply, which shows whole while the bar is open; a tool step's closed details and hidden action items are not. Elsewhere on the board ⌘F still focuses the search box. Rebind it as "Chat: Find" in Settings → Keyboard.
- The bookmark icon under a reply (title "Add this reply to my to-dos") saves it to My notes as a to-do named after its first line; the to-do's **Go to reply** scrolls the chat back to that reply, loading earlier turns if needed.
- /btw questions never enter the transcript: the answer shows as a card with Close (sends Esc), and the composer hides while it is open.
- In the terminal view a plain drag is Claude's own selection, and what Claude copies reaches the Mac clipboard, a remote session's too. Option+drag selects the terminal's text directly; Cmd+C copies it.

## Shell pane
❯ Shell opens a terminal in the session's work folder (its PR worktree if it has one, else the checkout), the same shell each time. Dot: green = live in the session's work, amber = live elsewhere, red = disconnected. The ▾ menu moves the shell into any PR worktree or the checkout, shows which other shells sit there, and has VS Code (moves an idle conversation to VS Code). Agents run things you watch here (the /shell/run call).

## Worktrees
Odin doesn't make worktrees; agents do. The board reads where each session really worked, for the repo pill and the shell menu. "Delete worktrees when their PR merges" (Settings → Storage → Cleanup, on by default) prunes every 10 minutes in repos with a .worktrees folder: uncommitted files wait 7 days and are then saved to refs/pruned/<name>; a worktree with no commits touched in the last 24 hours is never removed. Settings → Storage lists each worktree (about 3 GB each), ticks the ones already on main, and deletes them. The remote host has no pruner: Done on a remote session removes its worktree there at once (the branch stays; uncommitted files are saved to refs/pruned/<name>), and Resume gets it back from the branch.

## New Session dialog
The first line is the title. Paste, drop or **+ Attach** images, video, PDFs and .md or .txt files (the session reads them as files); "/" opens skills. "Repo: agent picks" takes a repo only when exactly one checkout matches what you typed, else the default folder. Model: Default / Fable / Opus / Sonnet / Haiku, kept for later launches. Start session also adds the same text to Tasks as a task, linked to the session the way Start on a task links them, so it shows on Tasks as Working with Go to session; Done on the session marks that task Done. Sessions that automations, the Night Agent or Auto-start begin add no task. The Backlog toggle turns the button into "Add to backlog" and sends it to Tasks instead (no images). Store: claudeCommand (model, toBacklog).

## Unsent messages are kept
What you type and haven't sent stays put until you send it: a session's reply box (per session), the New Session dialog, the quick-add task box, the compose box on Tasks and Mine, and Huginn's ask box. Leave, close the box with Cancel, reload the window or restart Odin (an update too), and the text is back the next time you open it. Sending clears it; Escape in the Tasks compose box clears it on purpose. It is saved on this computer as you type. Store: chatDrafts (localStorage "odin-chat-drafts", the 50 newest).

## Notifications
A macOS banner "<task> needs you" ("Waiting for your reply" or "Finished its turn") with a sound when a session moves into Needs you. Not for parked sessions, or when it is on screen in the focused window. Clicking it opens the session.

## Session History (rail, More menu)
Search the transcripts of every finished session Odin launched, by word or by who asked (person chips). Resume reopens one in its folder on the board. Claude Code deletes transcripts after 30 days.

## All (Tasks → All)
One list across every source in bands: **Now** (due, or asked in the last 4 hours), **Today** (reviews and asks waiting on you), **Later**. A task you add here is Now for 4 hours, then Today; a Low one goes to Later. **Up next** sits on top; drag to reorder. **Waiting on others** sits under it: a task blocked on someone or something else - a teammate, a PR, a date. Park one from its right-click menu ("Back on Up next tomorrow", "in 3 days", "in a week"), from the side panel ("Waiting on" - who or what: as you type it suggests the task's own person and everyone in your Slack workspace, or type anything, like a PR or a date - and the day it goes "back on Up next" - not its due date, then **Wait**), or tick rows and press **Waiting** (3 days). A parked task leaves Up next and the bands, its second line says who it waits on and when it comes back, and on that day Odin puts it back on top of Up next by itself with a note. The hourglass on the row, **Stop waiting** in the panel or the right-click menu brings it back early. A task Huginn or an automation adds through Odin's API can land straight on Up next: at the top, at the end, or at a place you name. Only tasks are listed: there is no list of sessions here. A task with a session running says Working on its own row, with Go to session; the header adds "N working" (tasks with a session running), and those tasks don't count in "waiting on you". Filters: Source, Priority, Place, Status, "Due or overdue only", "Show what's Done" (the graveyard: every row put away, done or dropped, newest first, each with Undo and Open; a row dropped by Review's sweep or a drop rule reads "dropped", with the reason on hover, and the toggle says "Done or Dropped" once there is one; below it, "Closed in Jira, GitHub or ClickUp - last 14 days" lists work related to you that closed in its own tool - a Jira ticket you're assigned or filed moved to Done, a PR you wrote or reviewed merged or closed, an issue assigned to you closed, a ClickUp task assigned to you done - each with its state, date and Open, fetched only while the toggle is on). Tick rows to Set status / Up next / Waiting / Done together.

## Settings
The sidebar shows: Connections, Sessions, Remote, Storage, Backlog & Night Agent, Notifications, Appearance, Keyboard, Advanced - one topic each, with what's on it written under its name. The search box above the list finds any setting by its label, its section or what it does, and scrolls to it. Store settingsScreen holds which one is open.

## Connections (Settings → Connections)
"The accounts Odin reads your work from" - Odin's integrations: Slack, GitHub, Jira, Notion, Gmail, ClickUp and Calendar. These are Odin's own, not MCP servers, so a session's or Huginn's MCP tool list never shows them. Sign-ins stay on this computer. Each row has Connect, Reconnect and Disconnect; disconnecting takes that source's items out of every tab. When a newer Odin asks Slack for more than your sign-in allows, the Slack row says "Odin needs new permissions - Reconnect"; Reconnect asks Slack for them. An expired GitHub, Jira or Notion sign-in renews itself, so a row says "signed out - sign in again" only when the sign-in itself is gone. Slack, Jira and Notion first ask for a Client ID and Secret when no OAuth app is set up; Calendar offers that under "Sign in with Google instead". Huginn reaches every connected service's full API with action=service.
- **Profiles**: each profile has its own accounts, board sessions, Slack queue and tasks; only the active one shows anywhere. Rename in place, Switch to, or add one. A deleted profile can be restored for 30 days (click Show deleted profiles under the list), then it is gone.
- **Claude**: "The account your sessions run on", for the whole Mac. Sign in / Reconnect opens the browser; paste the code if claude.ai shows one.
- **Slack** (read-only): the Slack feed. Set the Queue emoji (default eyes) and the Queue and start emoji (default robot_face).
- **GitHub**: your PRs and review requests, by device-code sign-in.
- **Jira** (read-only): your issues, in the Jira feed.
- **Notion**: a database's rows in the Notion feed. Odin sees only pages shared with it and their subpages; to add a teamspace, on its top page use ••• → Connections → Odin.
- **Gmail**: unread mail in the Email feed, by app password (needs 2-Step Verification), not OAuth.
- **ClickUp**: a personal API token (pk_…); sessions can read the ClickUp tasks they get, and Done on a ClickUp task closes it there.
- **Calendar**: Sign in with Google (read-only, the primary calendar). Outlook and iCloud connect by their secret iCal address instead ("Outlook or iCloud? Paste a secret iCal address"). A build with no Google app shows the iCal form first.

## Sessions settings (Settings → Sessions)
A session's life on this computer, top to bottom. Besides the launch gate limits ("When they start", below):
- **Where they start → Default folder**: where a session starts when no repo is picked. Unset: the folder your repos live in.
- **Where they start → Full Disk Access**: without it, macOS asks each time a session reads Music, Photos, Desktop or Documents.
- **On the board → Rename sessions automatically** (default on): renames each card once, after what it turned out to be about. Names you set are kept.
- **On the board → Compact conversations moved to Idle** (default off): /compact the moment a card is parked in Idle or waits there on others, while Claude's cache of it is still warm.
- **On the board → Close idle sessions after** (default 3 hours, 0 = never).
- **When they won't start → Restart Odin completely**: when every new session fails to start (for example "PTY process exited immediately"), the Restart Completely button asks first, then closes every open session and restarts Odin with fresh terminals. Sessions on a remote host keep running. The only way to do this on Windows and Linux; on macOS it is also Odin menu > Restart Odin Completely.

## Advanced (Settings → Advanced)
What a session runs, rarely changed:
- **Claude command**: what starts Claude, e.g. an alias for another account. One per line; with two or more, pick one in the top bar. Empty: claude. Store: claudeCommand.
- **Update plugins**: refreshes every plugin marketplace and moves each enabled plugin to its newest version, so a team runs the same skills. New sessions get the new versions; open ones keep theirs until they restart.
- **Default prompt**: added to the end of every task prompt (how to work, the Shell, ACTION ITEMS), with Reset to default. The board reads ACTION ITEMS from each reply. Store: sessionInstructions.

## Remote (Settings → Remote)
Your sessions when you're not at this computer.
- **Remote host**: run new sessions on an EC2 instance of yours over AWS Systems Manager, so they keep going with the Mac shut. Pick the AWS profile, region and instance (the list shows only instances tagged Odin = host in that profile and region; **Search again** asks AWS again, for a host made or tagged since the list loaded; Set up installs Claude, gh, tmux and git there; Use picks it; an AWS config with no default profile needs no change: the AWS profile picker shows the config's first profile and Odin uses it until you pick another), then the switch "Sessions start on <host>". Claude sign-in (the host gets its own; on Windows, Odin opens Claude's sign-in page and the section shows "Sign in to Claude for the host" with the link, a Copy button and a box, "Paste the code from the Claude page": approve in the browser, paste the code the page shows, and Submit), "API key at the plan limit" (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, or a custom key saved under it), "Connectors on the host" (Slack, Notion, Jira, GitHub, ClickUp tokens kept on the host; Gmail never goes; with Slack there, a session still reads a Slack thread while this Mac is asleep). Claude Code's own connectors (Slack, Notion and any other MCP server you signed in to in Claude Code here) work in sessions on the host with no sign-in there: Odin copies their sign-ins over with the other tokens, every half hour while this Mac is awake, until the host has a sign-in of its own for each (see Jira, Notion and GitHub below). The first copy can ask for Keychain access to "Claude Code-credentials"; choose Always Allow and "Claude setup on the host" (copies parts of ~/.claude there; Sync again after you change them). Both switch groups start with All, which flips every switch in the group.
- **Remote host → GitHub accounts on the host**: one switch per connected GitHub account - whether its token goes to the host with a session (and as the GitHub connector there). A session gets the account that owns its repo when that one may go, else the main one. A task on a repo owned by an account kept off the host starts on this computer. Default: the main connection only.
- **Remote host → Jira, Notion and GitHub sign in on the host by themselves**: their tokens expire, so the host gets a sign-in of its own and refreshes it, and they keep working there with this computer shut. Turning Jira or Notion on among the connectors on the host (a new host turns every connector on) opens that service's consent page in your browser, one after the other; approve each once. Turning a GitHub account on under GitHub accounts on the host starts its sign-in the same way (enter the code shown in the row). Until one is approved, the Connectors row says "<Jira or Notion> uses this computer's token until you approve it for the host" (that token runs out about an hour after this computer sleeps); click it to open the consent page again. Turning a connector off on the host removes its sign-in there. Claude Code's own connectors (Slack, Notion and any MCP server you signed in to in Claude Code here) work the same way: setting up a host opens each one's sign-in page in turn, and once you approve, the host's Claude Code keeps and refreshes a sign-in of its own. Until then the Connectors row says "Claude Code's <names> use this computer's sign-in until you approve them for the host"; click it to open their sign-in pages. Slack and ClickUp tokens in Odin's own connectors don't expire, so the host uses them as they are.
- **Auto-start new tasks**: one switch per source - Slack, Jira, GitHub, Notion, Tasks (all off by default), folded under **Sources (N on)**, collapsed until you click it. While sessions start on the remote host, a task new in a source switched on starts a session there by itself, with no click and no question, and a toast says "Auto-started from <source>". For Slack that is a message you react to with the Queue emoji (default :eyes:); its card wears the Auto-started pill. A source's tasks that already waited when Odin first looked are never started, and each task is started at most once. With the host off, nothing auto-starts. Store: odin.json autoStartSources.
- **Phone → Phone access**: the Odin iPhone app shows the board and answers sessions, on this Wi-Fi or through a relay, end to end encrypted. With it on, a QR code pairs a phone (once, for 10 minutes); paired phones are listed with Remove and Unpair all. In the iPhone app, a session's screen lists its PRs under the title, each with its state (Open, Approved, Merged, Closed) and CI, so you see whether it merged without opening the Brief; the Brief lists its links with a hide button on each; what you hide there or on the desktop brief is hidden on both, under Hidden (N), where show brings it back. With a remote host set up, a phone paired with this Mac also reaches the sessions on that host with the Mac closed or asleep - no second code. The phone then lists the host's sessions that were on the Mac's board when it went away and the ones started since, each under its real status; a session you marked Done on the Mac stays off. The Night Agent page and search inside conversations need the Mac, and say so then: "Your Mac is away, and only it can do this. Open Odin on your Mac." The phone's Backlog lists your own tasks no session was started on yet, like Next in line on the desktop: a task whose session ended is not listed again. A task the phone asks to start that can't start says why on the phone; added with Start now, it stays saved in Tasks. **Tasks with the Mac closed** (off by default, shown with a remote host set up): the top 15 of Next in line and your own Tasks are kept on the host, and the host also fetches your Slack (the Queue emoji), Jira, GitHub, Notion (the Tasks view's database) and ClickUp tasks itself every 2 minutes through the connectors on the host, less what you marked Done on the Mac. So with the Mac asleep the phone lists them, new ones included, and Start runs one on the host; New task on the phone adds one there too (with Start, it starts at once; files need the Mac), and Done on a session there ends it. When the Mac is back, the board picks those sessions up, a new task not started goes into Tasks, and what you marked Done on the phone is done on the Mac too. A source the host has no working sign-in for is left out (Jira, Notion and GitHub need their own sign-in on the host to keep working past about an hour); Gmail and Notion mentions never show there. They travel over the host's own encrypted connection and only your user on the host can read them; they stop listing after 3 days without the Mac, and turning it off deletes them from the host. In the iPhone app, an error line offers Reset pairing (the phone forgets Odin; scan the QR code again), Odin sends the app its newer screens and the app reloads into them by itself, with no button to tap; while something you typed or attached is not sent yet, it waits. A banner says when the app itself needs an update for Odin's newer screens (that one comes from Xcode or the store). While the board loads, the spinner names the step it waits on (reaching the Mac on this Wi-Fi or through the relay, the Mac checking the phone, the Mac's answer) and for how many seconds. Done or Remind me on the phone also marks the task the session was started on as done, as the board's Done does, so it does not come back to All tasks.
- **Claude app → Remote Control**: Off (default), Every session or Selected sessions. A session then shows in claude.ai/code and the Claude app as "[Odin] <task>". With Selected, turn it on per session from the card's right-click menu.

## Storage (Settings → Storage)
- **Worktrees**: every session worktree (about 3 GB each), ticked when its work already landed on main; Delete removes the ticked ones. See Worktrees above.
- **Cleanup → Delete worktrees when their PR merges** (default on), see Worktrees.
- **Backup → iCloud Drive**: your board, briefs and scrollback, copied daily, kept 14 days in iCloud Drive → Odin Backups. Off until you click Back up now once; Disconnect turns it off again and keeps the saved days. To restore, quit Odin and copy a day's files back into ~/.odin.

## Notifications settings (Settings → Notifications)
Desktop banners (macOS controls them; the button opens System Settings), Sound on or off and Volume, and Reminders → Notify at: the time "Remind me" sessions and due dates notify on their day, one notification per day.

## Appearance (Settings → Appearance)
Theme: pick one (built in: Odin Dark, Light, Monokai, Molokai), or follow the system with a Light theme and a Dark theme; Custom themes: theme files can be imported (256 KB at most). Text size (90% to 150%) makes Odin's own text bigger or smaller; the in-app browser keeps its size, while ⌘+/⌘- zoom everything. Sessions → Show sessions as a chat (default on), and Fold long replies (default off): off shows a long reply whole, with its TL;DR card still there; on, a latest reply too long for the window folds under its TL;DR card. Links → Open links inside Odin: links from the listed sites open in a panel over the page; each site has a switch; other links go to your default browser. Stores: theme (fn setTheme), inAppBrowser.

## Keyboard (Settings → Keyboard)
Shortcuts: search and re-record any shortcut; a key already in use offers Reassign; Reset all. From any app → "Double-tap to show or hide Odin": Record, then tap a modifier twice.

## Top bar and updates
The top bar has one status pill for Claude usage (5-hour and week), this computer (sessions, memory, CPU, free disk) and each remote host (sessions, memory, free disk). It shows only what is yellow or red, worst first, and a green dot when all is fine; hover lists everything. Click it when the disk is full to delete unused worktrees. Odin checks for a release every hour; a violet strip says "Odin X is available". Update and restart downloads it, quits and swaps in the new app; open terminal sessions and unsent messages survive the restart. If those sessions still run on an Odin signed differently (from before 1.1.0), macOS asks for folder access again and again, as "Odin" and as "Odin-<number>"; at launch Odin asks "Restart open sessions on this Odin?", and Restart Completely closes every open session and stops the prompts (the same as Odin menu > Restart Odin Completely). Later hides it until the next check. Nothing installs on its own.

## Huginn (the raven)
The raven button at the bottom of the rail, or ⌘⇧I. Until first opened it has a pulsing violet ring. The panel shows sample asks and a "Got a job for me?" box (images can be pasted); earlier chats can be resumed (a click) or deleted (the ✕ on hover), and New chat starts a fresh one. He runs on Opus with your Claude command, unless the model picker at the top right of his greeting (Fable / Opus / Sonnet / Haiku) says otherwise - kept for later chats (store: claudeCommand, key crowModel). In an open chat, the model picker in the chat itself switches it. Without Claude's task list or auto-memory, so a task goes to Odin's backlog and "always do X" becomes a rule. The conversation persists until ✓ Done. A turn silent too long counts as stuck and the next message resumes it in a fresh process. He is never a board card and takes no launch slot. A dot on the button means an answer, question or failure waits; it pulses while he works; his banner opens his panel. Once a week, when last week beat the week before on Insights (more merged PRs, or more agents running at once), he pops up a short "good job" note, and when last week was your most merged PRs ever he calls it a new all-time high; a week that didn't, he stays quiet (localStorage "odin-huginn-cheered").

## Automations (screen: Automations)
Three tabs: Schedules, Rules, Reminders.
- **Schedules**: tasks that start themselves on a schedule, only while Odin is open. Pick Repeat, day and time, or Custom for a cron. A run that came due while Odin was shut is skipped, not caught up. A due run starts even if the last run's session is still open; Pause stops it. "Run now" starts it at once. While the remote host is on, a schedule's "On <host>" / "On this Mac" button picks where its runs start; "On this Mac" keeps a job that needs this computer's files here. An optional tag makes it run only when an open session on the board has that tag; the run gets those sessions' paneIds and must drop the tag when done. Each run's card is tagged "automation". Schedules also show in My Tasks, marked amber with their schedule. Huginn adds one when you ask ("every weekday at 9, ...") and can change, pause or remove the existing ones. Store: tasks (an automation is a task with a cron).
- **Rules**: "When <situation>, <action>" - standing instructions for every session Odin starts from now on. A session already running, or one you resume, keeps the prompt it had. A rule can be pinned to some repos ("in"), or to every repo except those ("not in"), and paused. Rules about pull requests or pushes are also re-sent to the agent each time it opens or pushes to a PR. Store: rules - fn add(when, action, repos?, exclude?), update(id, patch), remove(id).
- **Reminders**: every snoozed session and dated feed row, soonest first. "Remind me to ... on ... at ..." at the top adds a reminder of your own with no task behind it; it fires at its own time and has Done instead of Open. Resume early, Move the day, or drop one. A reminder shows its whole text, which you can select and copy. Click a reminder's day-and-time chip ("Tomorrow at 09:00") to change its day or hour in place; it saves as you pick, and OK closes it. A snoozed session shows on the board only when it is due. Store: reminders.

## Night Agent (Settings → Backlog & Night Agent, or the Night Agent page's header)
"Work the backlog overnight": inside the window (default 23:00 to 07:00) it starts the top task of Next in line, waits until that session stops working, then starts the next. One at a time, up to the night's cap (default 8 sessions).
- Rows you pick for tonight (up to 20) run first, in order. After them it stops, or goes on with the ranked queue (the "after picks" choice).
- Only Tasks, Slack, Jira, GitHub and Notion rows can run. Email, ClickUp and Calendar rows can't.
- The AI ranks Next in line using your "How to sort it" words and the Night Agent instructions, so "don't include X" in either keeps X out. It re-ranks when the words change or new rows arrive.
- Its Instructions are added to every Night Agent session's prompt. The default says: work alone, open a PR, but send nothing other people see (no Slack, email, comments, merges or deploys).
- It runs only on power: on battery it starts nothing. Odin keeps the Mac from idle sleep during the window, but a closed lid still sleeps it. It runs only while Odin is open.
- On a remote host, a runner there (a timer, every minute) starts the night's sessions one at a time, so they run with the laptop closed. It holds the next one while the host has under a quarter of its memory free or its load is above its core count - the rule Odin itself applies before a launch to the host - and looks again a minute later. The hold is a line in the host's journal for the runner's unit (odin-night-<user>). When the laptop's queue runs out, the host picks from the Slack messages you marked :eyes: itself, and skips every one that is Done, already has a session (running or finished, started by hand, auto-start or a night) or repeats one that does.
- Its log says when each window opened and closed, what it started, and why it started nothing (off, battery, Odin shut or asleep, cap reached, waiting on a session, everything ruled out, ranking failed). The Night Agent page groups it by night, newest first, and its Last night card lists the sessions the last window started with each one's state (Working, Needs you, Final Review, Idle, Done). Read the log with action=night.
- Store: nightAgent (offHours: enabled, start, end, maxSessions, instructions, picked, afterPicks; nightLog).

## One by one
Huginn can line up backlog tasks to run one after another: each starts when the last stops working. Odin drives the line. Store: oneByOne (ids left; set ids to [] to stop).

## Personalization settings (Settings → Personalization)
- **Your voice** ("Write drafts in my voice"): on by default. Every session starts with the guide Odin learns from your Slack messages written into its instructions, so drafts you send as yourself sound like you. Odin retrains on its own - daily from your sent messages, after every 15 new ones it sees, and from how you rewrote a session's drafts before sending - using counted numbers (length, capitals, periods, Hebrew, emoji) and where each message went. Messages you wrote to a bot (one that mentions a bot, or a DM with one) are left out, and the next training drops the ones it kept before. A switch turns it off; Train now retrains at once; the guide is shown, and Corrections (one per line) always win over it. Under a draft in a session's chat, beside Copy and Tune voice, an **In <language>** button (for example In Hebrew) asks the session to redraft it in that language; the buttons list only the languages you write in on Slack (English, plus any other one in at least 5% of your messages), never the draft's own.
- **Slack → Let sessions send Slack messages** (default off): off, sessions can't send or schedule a Slack message as you, on this computer or on a remote host - Odin blocks the Slack send and schedule tools in Claude's settings and allows the draft tool, so every message waits in Slack for you to read and send. On, Odin lifts that block (here at once, on a remote host at its next session start) and a session can send or schedule a message as you when you ask it to; sessions still draft by default.

## Launch gate and Queued (Settings → Sessions → When they start)
A started session waits in Idle under "Queued", with a reason, until all limits allow it. Nothing is refused - the card exists from the start. Changes apply within 5 seconds.
- **Hold new sessions when this computer is** over a CPU load (default 70%).
- **Hold new sessions when free memory is under** (default 2 GB).
- **Hold new sessions when free disk is under** (default 10 GB). Each session takes a worktree of about 3 GB. 0 means no limit.
- **Hold new sessions when this many are working** (default 5). Sessions waiting on you don't count. 0 means no limit.
- **One session per repo at a time** (default off): on, a session in a repo another one is working in waits until that one stops. Off lets them run side by side, each in its own worktree.
- Huginn takes no slot and holds no checkout.
- Store: launchLimits (hostCpuPercent, minFreeMemoryGb, minFreeDiskGb, maxWorkingAgents, oneSessionPerCheckout; fn setLimits).

## Insights (screen: Insights)
What you got done and what it cost, week by week. Plain counts from session transcripts, no AI call. Panels: What you did, Shipped per week, Time with agents (your time vs agent time), Where the hours went (by repo, a repo can be hidden), Where work comes from (source), Who asks, Whose work you ran, Your queue, Improvements. Move between weeks with Previous week / Next week.
`;

/** The sections, each with its heading. */
const sections = () =>
	ODIN_GUIDE.split(/\n(?=## )/).filter((part) => part.startsWith("## "));

/** The whole guide, or only the sections that mention `q`. */
export function odinGuide(q?: string): string {
	const word = q?.trim().toLowerCase();
	if (!word) return ODIN_GUIDE;
	const hits = sections().filter((part) => part.toLowerCase().includes(word));
	return hits.length
		? hits.join("\n\n")
		: `No section mentions "${q}". Sections: ${sections()
				.map((part) => part.split("\n")[0].slice(3))
				.join(", ")}.`;
}
